{"from": "text:tacitus-histories-4-81", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:jn-09-06-spittle-remedy", "grade": "C", "note": "Tacitus, Hist. 4.81: an Alexandrian blind man begged Vespasian 'to moisten his cheeks and eye-balls with his spittle' - saliva applied to eyes as a healing gesture in the period. It attests the gesture's currency, never the healing; a parallel from elsewhere in the empire."}
{"from": "text:mishnah-shabbat-7-2", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:jn-09-14-sabbath-clay", "grade": "B", "note": "'One who kneads dough' stands among the thirty-nine primary categories of Sabbath labour, after grinding and sifting. Kneading clay on the Sabbath fell under the same rule. Redacted c. 200 CE; the category may not describe the practice of the 30s CE exactly."}
{"from": "site:pool-of-siloam", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:jn-09-07-pool-of-siloam", "grade": "A", "note": "The Second Temple period pool rediscovered in 2004 (Shukron and Reich, Israel Antiquities Authority): stone steps on at least three sides, 225 ft wide, at the southern end of the City of David - a large public pool at Siloam in the period."}
{"from": "text:josephus-war-5-siloam", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:jn-09-07-pool-of-siloam", "grade": "A", "note": "Josephus (War 5.140-145) names Siloam as a fountain 'which hath sweet water in it, and this in great plenty also' at Jerusalem's south, first century."}
{"from": "artifact:coins-pool-of-siloam", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:jn-09-07-pool-of-siloam", "grade": "B", "note": "Coins of Alexander Jannaeus embedded in the plaster lining give the pool a secure earliest construction date of 103-76 BCE - the Second Temple setting of the washing."}
{"from": "artifact:byzantine-pool-of-siloam", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:jn-09-07-pool-of-siloam", "grade": "D", "note": "The 5th-century Byzantine pool at the tunnel's end was wrongly thought to be the New Testament pool until the 2004 discovery; the identification it carries is the superseded one, recorded D as the brief requires."}
{"from": "artifact:siloam-inscription", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:jn-09-07-pool-of-siloam", "grade": "C", "note": "The 8th-century BCE inscription documents the waterworks bringing Gihon water to the Siloam pool area - the older water system behind the name, not the first-century pool itself."}
{"from": "text:birkat-ha-minim", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:jn-09-22-aposynagogos", "grade": "D", "note": "J. L. Martyn (1968) linked the curse on the minim to John's expulsion passages (9:22; 12:42; 16:1); but the benediction's date is disputed (Second Temple to c. 135 CE hypotheses; c. 85-120 CE commonest) and no formal ban (niddui or herem) is evidenced before the 3rd century. Grade D: the identification and date are disputed; it may not attest a 30s CE decree."}
{"from": "text:talmud-shabbat-55a", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:jn-09-02-blindness-blamed-on-sin", "grade": "C", "note": "Rav Ami: 'There is no death without sin... and there is no suffering without iniquity' - affliction read as the fruit of sin inside the same rabbinic culture. Amoraic saying, Talmud redacted c. 500 CE; it may not state the custom of the 30s CE exactly."}
{"from": "text:4q521-2", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:jn-09-32-never-heard", "grade": "C", "note": "A pre-Christian Qumran text expects the anointed one's age to bring 'giving sight to the blind'. It attests the messianic expectation the narrative invokes, not the man's historical statement that no one had ever healed a born-blind man."}
{"from": "artifact:coins-pool-of-siloam", "type": "found_at", "to": "site:pool-of-siloam"}
{"from": "claim:jn-09-06-spittle-remedy", "type": "related_to", "to": "claim:mk-08-23-spittle-two-stage", "note": "John's Jesus makes mud with saliva and anoints the eyes once; Mark 8:23 uses spittle alone in two stages with no clay - the same healing-gesture family, different procedure."}
{"from": "claim:jn-09-07-sight-restored", "type": "related_to", "to": "claim:mt-09-27-son-of-david", "note": "Matthew 9:27-31 has two blind men healed in Galilee; John 9 one man born blind at Jerusalem via Siloam - different healings, not the same event."}
{"from": "claim:jn-09-07-sight-restored", "type": "related_to", "to": "claim:mk-10-46-47-bartimaeus", "note": "Mark 10:46-52 heals blind Bartimaeus at Jericho; John 9's man is congenitally blind and healed through washing in Siloam - different events."}
{"from": "claim:jn-09-07-sight-restored", "type": "related_to", "to": "claim:lk-18-35-43-blind-beggar-jericho", "note": "Luke 18:35-43 heals a blind beggar near Jericho; John 9's man is born blind and healed at Siloam - different events."}
{"from": "claim:jn-09-08-sat-and-begged", "type": "related_to", "to": "claim:mk-10-46-47-bartimaeus", "note": "Both men are known as blind beggars: John's man 'sat and begged' (9:8), Mark's Bartimaeus is a blind beggar at Jericho."}
{"from": "claim:jn-09-14-sabbath-clay", "type": "related_to", "to": "claim:mt-12-10-sabbath-healing-debate", "note": "John 9:14-16 disputes making clay (kneading) and healing on the Sabbath; Matthew 12:10 raises the same lawfulness question over a withered hand."}
{"from": "claim:jn-09-22-aposynagogos", "type": "related_to", "to": "claim:mt-10-17-councils-synagogues", "note": "Matthew 10:17 expects flogging in synagogues before councils; John 9:22 a standing agreement to expel confessors from the synagogue - exclusion rather than scourging."}
{"from": "claim:jn-09-29-disciples-of-moses", "type": "related_to", "to": "text:deuteronomy-34-10", "note": "Deuteronomy 34:10: 'there has not arisen a prophet in Israel like Moses, whom Yahweh knew face to face' - the scriptural basis of the leaders' appeal to Moses. Cross-reference only, not external evidence."}
